WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:11.180
thank you very much. May we then move on to
the next item, which is item five, on the

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:16.240
Consolidated 2014/2015 Financial Report, month
two. Paul?

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.960
Paul Baumann: Thank you. I do not have much
to add to Barbara's comments about the planning.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.289
Inevitably, the finances are the end product
of the planning and the consequence of all

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:28.849
the things that have been described. I also
do not have much, greatly, to say about month

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:33.370
two. This is because, as those of you who
have been with the NHS for any length time

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.940
will know, by month two you know virtually
nothing about the year. There is very little

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.380
data coming through in the first month or
so.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:46.380
I would like to maybe bring the various discussions
of risk we have just talked about to a kind

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.400
of conclusion. As we know, we exited last
year with an £800 million surplus. However,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.120
it was £400 million less surplus than we
went into last year with. Already, therefore,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:02.360
in last year's performance we saw the seeds,
if you like, of many of the challenges we

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.660
have this year. Specialised commissioning
is the biggest one of those.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.270
That pattern is absolutely reflected in the
plans you have described in front of you.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.790
We have been facing significant challenges.
I do not think I have ever been quite this

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.110
late in the year without a set of plans which
are all, kind of, laid to rest and ready to

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.280
be acted upon. That, I think, is just an indication
of the difficulties different health economies

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.280
have been facing around the country. It is
not just in specialised commissioning, but

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.300
in other parts of the forest as well.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:39.680
At the time of writing the paper, you will
see that there is £137 million lower surplus

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:45.150
being planned, by the various parts of the
organisation, than we need to deliver this

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.560
year. To put it another way: we have £400
million of draw down of prior year surpluses

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.900
available to us. However, this plan would
have implied that we needed £537 million.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.330
There is no source of additional money that
we can fall back on for that.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:02.960
However, I just need to put that in context,
of course. That is about 0.1% of our total

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.690
budget. Sometimes I think we can slightly
overstate the significance of what we are

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.580
doing. That is a very, very small difference.
However, it is one that we have needed to

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.510
resolve.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.209
I am pleased to say that has resolved, how
we are going to close that gap in specialised,

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:24.319
as Barbara said. We have also resolved how
we are going to close that gap in the CCG

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:27.859
position. Over the next few days I am expecting
plans from the very small number of organisations

00:02:27.859 --> 00:02:33.659
who were not previously fulfilling the expectation
that we would not draw down CCG surpluses,

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:40.180
in aggregate, in 2014/2015. We also expected
that we would seek to keep any deficits that

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.469
were inevitably the case at the level they
were in 2013/2014, rather than allowing those

00:02:44.469 --> 00:02:51.469
to increase. There were about 18 CCGs around
the country whose plans did not fulfil that

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.889
pattern. However, by the end of this week
or early next week we should have plans in

00:02:57.889 --> 00:03:00.769
which will close that gap.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:04.269
That will enable us at last to move beyond
the planning cycle. It has seemed to go on

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:06.969
forever. However, we will be able to move
into the much more important piece of work

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:11.950
which we now have to do, which is delivering
against those plans. It is inevitably the

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.059
case that this is the riskiest set of plans
we have had for a while. Last year at this

00:03:16.059 --> 00:03:20.299
time of the year there was a large proportion
of the draw down available to us which we

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:25.870
had not allocated. It was ready to cover risks
as they arose during the year. We needed some

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.659
of it. Some of it led to a slightly higher
surplus at the end of last year than we had

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:36.029
anticipated. This year, all of that draw down,
all of the £400 million of money from previous

00:03:36.029 --> 00:03:40.829
years, has had to be allocated in coming to
the plans we have talked about. There is not

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:46.019
some kind of central place to which we can
go for risk mitigation as we go through. As

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:49.010
we have talked about, and I will highlight
in a moment, there are a number of areas of

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.659
risk we will be encountering as the year goes
by.

00:03:52.659 --> 00:03:57.499
So, we have all of the standard things. We
have contingencies in place of 0.5% across

00:03:57.499 --> 00:04:03.989
the piece. That has always been the case in
the NHS. Particularly in CCGs, we have a substantial

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:10.989
proportion of the 2% that we like them to
put aside for non recurrent purposes and transformation.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.329
Therefore, we have some risk mitigation. However,
it is very limited and none of it is held

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:17.970
centrally.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.509
That really means we are going to have to
focus a lot of attention on the risk assessment

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:28.060
and the mitigation assessment we do. We discussed
the management information pack with the finance

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.220
and investment committee at length. I bring
that pack to you, as the Board, in summarised

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.289
form. You will be aware that we have, each
month when we do the forecast, also the risks

00:04:35.289 --> 00:04:39.300
and mitigations against that. I am going to
suggest we spend a bit more time, as the year

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.990
goes by, looking at that particular set of
analyses. That will allow us to act quickly

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.610
when things are looking tight.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:52.610
There are two obvious things which sit at
the top of my mind as being the risk factors

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.650
in all of this. We have just discussed one
of them at length: specialised commissioning.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:02.090
I therefore will not repeat that. However,
the other one is the trends which Simon talked

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:07.129
about in the beginning of this meeting. That
is the emerging, apparent trend, at least,

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:11.300
of higher levels of activity, particularly
emergency activity than we had last year.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.550
They are certainly higher levels than we had
in the plan for this year. I think it is a

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:19.220
bit early days yet to know how much of that
is really going to translate into real trends

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.860
on the ground. How much of it is an artefact
of a couple of months of data, in one form

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.750
or another? As you can imagine, we are spending
a lot of time getting under the skin of that

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:32.430
and working out the answer to that question.
However, I would have to sound a bit of a

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:37.780
note of caution. For if that starts to become
a trend of any magnitude, as the year goes

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:44.439
by, that will start impacting on the financial
risk in the system.

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:47.939
I think that is probably as much as I wanted
to say on it, Malcolm. It is fairly clear

00:05:47.939 --> 00:05:49.569
otherwise, I think, from the report.

00:05:49.569 --> 00:05:50.389
Malcolm Grant: Simon?

00:05:50.389 --> 00:05:56.599
Simon Stevens: Paul, could you say a little
for the Board about the £2.1 billion of quick

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:01.680
efficiency savings that are assumed in order
to get to a balanced plan. That is obviously

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.159
a very substantial efficiency assumption.
Therefore, could you talk a bit about the

00:06:06.159 --> 00:06:12.500
mode of delivery and the confidence that we
have about the likelihood of that all being

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.830
realised. Barbara may want to add to that.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.810
Paul Baumann: I think it comes in two parts.
I would like to give you a bit of context.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.509
We have a plan for £2.1 billion. We had a
plan for 2013/2014 which was, I think, about

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:28.020
£50 million - £60 million less than that.
It was around about two point something billion.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.340
The level of ambition in the plan is, I would
say, to all intents and purposes, similar

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.550
to what we had last year. As we know, we undershot
the plan last year by about 15% in total.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.909
That left us about £300 million short. If
you want a simple way of saying what the risk

00:06:41.909 --> 00:06:44.560
is, if you like, around the QIPP, that would
have to be it.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.590
We have been through an extensive piece of
assurance with each of the CCG plans. Barbara

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.629
described that briefly in her patch. That
was to see the extent to which those QIPP

00:06:54.629 --> 00:07:00.870
plans have substance. The particular increases
in the two parts of the country where we have

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:05.180
struggled most, in terms of financial delivery
and QIPP delivery, are Midlands and East and

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:10.990
the South. There is arguably more pressure
on the system there from a financial perspective.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:15.949
It is worth saying that the CCGs, despite
under delivering on QIPP last year, were able

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:21.080
to deliver the financial bottom lines in aggregate
that we did deliver last year. They therefore

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.129
have mitigations for it. However, I would
say that the QIPP plans are as strong, if

00:07:24.129 --> 00:07:27.669
not stronger, this year than they were last
year. Of course, all the benefits of integrated

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:31.370
care and the work we are doing in that area
will start to feed through in the CCG plans.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:36.719
To be frank, the much bigger concern I have
is the £100 million additional stretch in

00:07:36.719 --> 00:07:42.509
the specialised commissioning plans. That
is part of that £2.1 billion that you talk

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:47.139
about. It is around about £100 million more
than we had in the QIPP delivery last year.

00:07:47.139 --> 00:07:52.789
It is intrinsically tied up with whether we
can start to have an impact on the level of

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:57.349
growth in activity in specialised commissioning.
That growth is running at an unaffordable

00:07:57.349 --> 00:08:01.139
rate for the scope of those services. It is
also tied up with whether we can get a better

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:08.139
grip on the basics of contracting, challenge
of activity and all the rest of it than we

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.189
had last year. That will also be a part of
that.

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:15.180
It is a big risk. I think we have to face
that. However, on the CCG side there is more

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.849
strength in the plans and probably greater
mitigation if some of it does not quite translate

00:08:19.849 --> 00:08:24.319
into delivery. On the specialised commissioning
front, there is a big stretch and little mitigation

00:08:24.319 --> 00:08:26.009
if it does not work.

00:08:26.009 --> 00:08:27.020
Malcolm Grant: Ed?

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:32.640
Ed Smith: Moira and I have been through this
is quite a lot of detail in the finance and

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.250
investment committee. Paul and I have also
had a number of bilaterals. I would say, absolutely,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:44.400
that there are greater risks in this plan.
There are lower levels of contingency than

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.530
I personally would like to see, as a Board
member. However, we are where we are on that.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:54.450
The only other point I would make is that
it is the end of June. We need to get into

00:08:54.450 --> 00:09:01.450
a rhythm of having a balanced budget on the
1st April, rather than at the end of the first

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:10.730
quarter. I know it has been massively complicated
and difficult. I am sympathetic. However,

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.530
from a perspective of running the organisation,
notwithstanding the difficulties, we ought

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:21.650
to be able to do it for the start of the year.
That is meant to be helpful, not unhelpful.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.800
It is not a criticism of the challenges you
have gone through.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.000
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Are there any other
observations? Ciaran?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.530
Ciaran Devane: This is a corollary to my other
question, I suppose. Next year will be harder.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:42.530
We are still ageing as a population, etc..
Is there enough change in the activity in

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:51.330
the plan? Are we moving towards new models
of care? This is not ultimately about financial

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:59.140
planning and it is not about budgets. Ultimately,
this is about change on the ground.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:08.950
Simon Stevens: Ciaran, I think you are absolutely
right. I think there is a sense across the

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:20.260
NHS that, actually, more of the same has nearly
run its course. There is still opportunity

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:26.560
out there. We know there are still differences
in efficiency in different parts of the health

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.460
service. However, the fact is people are up
for something different.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:36.460
Therefore, as you know, between now and the
autumn we are working with partners to articulate

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:42.400
a five year Forward View on new types of clinical
models, different ways of working and how

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.340
we can get there. I think that is precisely
to put flesh on the bones of the challenge

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:52.330
that you have, rightly, laid out. There is
an appetite for that. There are new ways of

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:56.590
working breaking out around the country. However,
that is often despite the system, not because

00:10:56.590 --> 00:11:00.370
of it. Our task is to identify what needs
to change to actually ensure that can come

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:04.400
about. That means changes both in the level
of local support and also the broader regulatory

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.460
and commissioning superstructure, if you like.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.860
Malcolm Grant: Noel?

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:17.860
Noel Gordon: As Ed said, going into the year
with no contingency on a budget with this

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:26.710
scale is complex, to say the least. However,
what is going on underneath the budget is

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:36.150
that there is a run cost for the NHS and then
there is a change cost for the NHS. Both are

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:45.010
rising. That clearly creates compression in
the budget system. You will have a view, Paul,

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:50.520
on what the relative balance is between those
two mega factors, if you like and how they

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:51.850
will change over time.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:58.850
However, let us look beyond this financial
year to some point of exit in the future.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:05.580
At what point do the run costs stabilise to
the point where we are creating capacity in

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.120
the system for investment in the change?

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.880
Simon Stevens: Not in our lifetimes.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:17.120
Noel Gordon: Is that a subject we should come
back to at a later point because it is fairly

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:17.180
complex question?

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.200
Paul Baumann: It is a complex question. It
is one to which there is not a short term

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.730
answer. That is a problem, because we have
to be doing the long term things. In a normal

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:29.310
circumstance you would want to be doing the
long term things when you have lots of money

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:36.310
to do it with. We have to do the long term
things at a time when money is compressed.

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:43.490
It is also fair to say the system is very
lumpy, in that sense. There are parts of the

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:49.160
country and individual organisations, including
individual CCGs, who have the financial capacity

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:53.760
to make the change costs, at least, work.
They can, potentially, run a bit ahead with

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.710
some of that. There are other parts of the
country and parts of the portfolio where people

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:01.240
are really struggling just to keep up with
the run costs. Specialised is the obvious

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:05.660
one. In those cases, their ability to do the
very things which would get them to the point

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.380
you have just described is severely impaired.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.270
Noel Gordon: So you are saying that both are
moving targets?

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:09.570
Paul Baumann: Yes. Exactly right.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:16.570
Moira Gibb: There certainly is not, Malcolm,
any money sitting at the centre to support

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:24.080
change. I think people in the system often
assume they can apply for some funds. We have

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:29.190
to find ways in which the whole organisation
is committing to achieving those changes.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:34.340
However, we need to dispel the idea that there
is a pot of money in Paul's pocket to facilitate.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:38.290
Simon Stevens: Absolutely. We need to dispel
that idea. However, as we think out over the

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:45.290
next three to five years, we need to identify
what kind of transformational capacity is

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.470
going to be required to get us to where we
need to get to. That is transformational capacity

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.670
both in terms of funding and other change
support. I think, as we were saying in relation

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:56.480
to Ciaran's question, more of the same is
not going to work. Therefore, to will the

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:00.170
ends is to will the means. The means are going
to have to be some kind of transformational

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.910
resourcing and intervention.

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:03.400
Ciaran Devane: Multi year.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:04.520
Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.080
Victor Adebowale: I guess this relates to
Ciaran's point really. I do not suppose there

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:15.080
is an answer. It is about knowing the pace
of the changes on the ground that are accumulating

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:23.340
so fast. It is not just that these changes
are taking place and we are welcoming them.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:27.060
That is actually happening in spite of us.
People are actually seeing that they are not

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:30.790
carrying on doing what they have always done[?].
However, I would like to have a sense of whether

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:37.790
that is growing at a pace that allows us to
make some decisions. Are we still going to

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:42.880
be here in five years' time with two or three
people doing the right things and making the

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:48.780
right changes that save money and improve
patient care? Or is it going to be half the

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:52.070
CCGs? I would like to get a sense of the pace
of change.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:55.530
Simon Stevens: Without wanting to be apocalyptic
about it, my answer to you, Victor, would

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.370
be that if it is two or three people in five
years' time we will not actually be here.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.050
Victor Adebowale: We are in trouble, yes.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:03.850
Simon Stevens: The reality is, what we are
currently doing is not sustainable, given

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:08.370
the likely funding envelope we are going to
have to operate in over that next three to

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:08.780
five years.

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:14.190
Victor Adebowale: Can I just follow that up?
In my view, the money to support [inaudible]

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:20.690
is not as important as how we support the
culture of change. That is much more about

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:27.690
values and the culture stuff which we rarely
talk about here. It does concern me that we

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.820
have a kind of structure with the leadership
academy and all that kind of stuff. However,

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:38.000
we need to align that around patient involvement
and community engagement so that they meet

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:45.000
in this single idea of getting accelerating
change. I do not have a sense of that. However,

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:52.730
it seems to me to be pretty critical. It is
more a statement than a question.

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.220
Malcolm Grant: Tim?

00:15:55.220 --> 00:16:02.220
Tim Kelsey: When you look at other sectors,
one of the challenges has been for the leadership

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:07.350
to take a very big gulp when it starts to
think about the level of investment that will

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:12.980
be required to enable the sort of disruptive
transformation we are talking about. The discontinuity

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:18.820
issue is a really big one for us, as a somewhat
fragmented system. How much will it cost to

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:23.980
go from here to there? There needs to be a
level of investment in doing that. I think

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:25.120
that is quite a difficult challenge.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.370
Victor Adebowale: Or there needs to be a shift
in current resourcing. That is not just in

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:33.630
NHS England, but also in all the supporting
bodies. It is not so much whether we have

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:39.730
enough money, it is whether we are spending
the money we have well.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.300
Malcolm Grant: On that uplifting note, Victor
-

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:44.900
Victor Adebowale: I think it is pretty uplifting
[inaudible].

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:49.310
Malcolm Grant: - and before we move onto the
next item, which is the performance report,

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:55.000
I think we have to summarise. Although we
have heard it every year, I think this really

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:01.260
is an immensely serious and challenging time.
The indicators we have are all that activity

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:08.260
is rising and costs are rising against activity.
We need to find new ways of treating patients

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:15.600
in different contexts and environments. We
also need a further upstream investment in

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:22.600
models of care. We spent some time yesterday
informally thinking through new models of

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:28.439
care and new financial means of supporting
them. However, I think Victor's point is absolutely

00:17:28.439 --> 00:17:35.120
right. You do not try and steer a system simply
by shifting sums of money around at the margins.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:42.120
The critical thing is the values and the behaviours
and the cultures which need to change in order

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:49.460
to be able to regenerate a model of care that
is fit for the 21st Century.

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:54.790
We will all be anxious, of course, to regularly
review performance in year. We do not know

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:58.520
whether some of the trends that you have identified,
and are identified in the next paper, are

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:04.690
short term or longer term and ingrained. However,
at the same time we will do whatever we can

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:11.690
to support CCGs and others in devising new
models of care. This is against the backcloth

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:18.480
probably the most stringent financial settlement
that the NHS has seen in many years.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:18.960
Right.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:24.060
Margaret Casely Hayford: Chairman, before
you leave this item, Paul mentioned that there

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:31.060
were a few reports outstanding. I think we
do not have another Board meeting to approve

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:36.900
the final report. Therefore, we will probably
need to delegate the approval.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:40.380
Paul Baumann: Indeed. Well, we will be picking
that up with the finance and investment committee

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:45.560
on a monthly basis anyway. So, yes, it is
a very helpful reminder. Once we have the

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:51.140
final plans in, we were looking for the finance
and investment committee, to be able to - I

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:55.370
am not sure which verb to use now in the new
structure. Approved is not quite the right

00:18:55.370 --> 00:19:00.710
word. However, they will endorse the sum of
the planning. They will accept the planning

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:05.220
numbers as our final plan. Thank you, that
is very helpful.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:10.140
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Before I move on, could
I just reinforce something I said earlier?

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:15.070
The finance team have done an outstanding
job in closing the accounts for last year.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:21.030
They also worked with colleagues around the
table in producing an annual report. I think

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:27.960
that report is very clear in understanding
what the NHS in England has achieved in the

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:34.260
last year against our mandate and our financial
performance. This will be published after

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:39.210
it has been laid before Parliament. All I
can do this morning, I think, is express,

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:45.410
once again on behalf of the Board, our grateful
thanks to Paul, his team and Ed and the audit

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:49.930
committee. In particular I would like to express
thanks, again, to the CCGs. This has been

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:56.930
an extraordinarily complex exercise. It is
quite remarkable it has come in as efficiently

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:59.420
and swiftly as it has.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:19:59.750
Thank you.

